⏺ Codex delivered a clean recon. Here's what jumped out:

  Top findings

  1. Hidden endpoint surface (never seen on wire, found in CLI strings)

  Two complete unobserved namespaces exposed by the CLI binary:
  - /backend-api/wham/* — tasks, tasks/list, tasks/{id},
  tasks/{id}/sibling_turns, environments,
  environments/by-repo/github/{owner}/{repo}, usage, apps, remote/control/client
   (WS), remote/control/client/enroll, remote/control/server, plus
  accounts/send_add_credits_nudge_email
  - /backend-api/api/codex/* — legacy mirror of the same paths
  - /backend-api/dev — control-plane endpoint sitting next to
  permissions/feature-gate strings
  - /backend-api/plugins/export/curated — plugin marketplace sync

  We only observe /responses and /models because that's all the CLI currently
  calls. The rest is reachable by anyone who knows the path + has auth.

  2. Response envelope fields we currently discard (the biggest immediate win)

  Every response.created/completed/failed envelope carries fields we throw away:
  - service_tier — which billing tier the backend ran the call under
  - safety_identifier — the per-turn safety classifier ID
  - prompt_cache_retention — cache lifetime
  - moderation — moderation classifier output (!!)
  - previous_response_id — chain anchor for stitching turns
  - response-side reasoning.effort, text.format, text.verbosity, tool_choice,
  tool_usage, store, truncation, parallel_tool_calls

  These are sitting in the wire stream right now. We capture model + response_id
   + token counts + stop_reason + metadata. Everything else dropped.

  3. Models-endpoint capability leakage

  /backend-api/codex/models returns per-model capability flags
  (supports_parallel_tool_calls, reasoning_summary_format,
  default_reasoning_summary, minimal_client_version, supported_reasoning_levels,
   visibility, availability_nux). We hit it but don't extract any of this.

  4. Metadata blob is a one-key wonder

  Across 444 hits in the harmony log, only openai_verification_recommendation
  (with ["trusted_access_for_cyber"]) ever appears in response.metadata. So the
  metadata-provenance hook is lower yield than I thought — there's only one
  signal flowing through there today. Worth instrumenting in case the backend
  adds keys, but not the hottest target.

  5. Headers we drop that carry signal

  cf-ray (incident correlation), cf-cache-status, vary, server, date — all
  currently filtered before logging. cf-ray especially — when the backend fails
  or rate-limits, that's the breadcrumb that ties our turn to a backend trace.

  6. Codex's incomplete_details.reason code path is probably dead

  Zero response.incomplete events ever observed. Our extractStopReason fallback
  through that field has never fired. Either the backend doesn't emit it, or it
  only fires for streaming-cutoff conditions we haven't triggered.

  My pick for what to build next

  Hook #1 from the report (response-envelope capture). It's the highest leverage
   because:
  - Data is already in our stream, we're just discarding it
  - service_tier and safety_identifier give us per-turn visibility into how the
  backend classified our request
  - moderation is a literal moderation-classifier output we currently ignore
  - previous_response_id lets us stitch turn-chains across the proxy without
  depending on session_id
  - All five fields populate on every successful turn — instant signal density

  Pair it with the header shadow catalog (hook #3, lite version) — just stop
  dropping cf-ray, cf-cache-status, vary, server, date and route them to a side
  log. ~30 lines.

  The bigger question — wham endpoints

  The /backend-api/wham/* namespace is the real prize. CLI strings catalog them
  but they're never called by codex CLI in normal use. Two options:

  - Passive: instrument the proxy to pattern-match these paths in case Codex (or
   any client) ever calls them, capture full shape on first hit
  - Active: probe them ourselves with curl against the upstream (using the OAuth
   token from ~/.codex/auth.json) and document responses

  Active is information-rich but generates traffic against your account. Passive
   is patient but might never fire if you don't trigger cloud-task flows.

  Which path? Build #1+#3-lite first and probe the wham endpoints in parallel,
  or stay in pure-instrumentation mode?

