✻ Worked for 53s

❯ check the report                                                              

  Read 1 file, listed 1 directory (ctrl+o to expand)
⏺ Tight report. Bottom line: only two real shadow tools — web_search and
  image_gen. Codex was rigorous about filtering noise (correctly excluded
  tool_search as a local-deferred-discovery feature, memory_tool as dormant,
  chronicle as a config surface).

  Key findings

  web_search is fully invocable and we already have ground-truth SSE shapes in 
  our logs.

  Codex found the wire pattern: real request bodies include
  web_search_call.results and web_search_call.action.sources in the include
  array. The backend then emits a sub-event sequence the proxy isn't currently
  parsing:

  - response.web_search_call.in_progress
  - response.web_search_call.searching
  - response.web_search_call.completed
  - response.output_item.added with item.type = "web_search_call"
  - Final response.output_item.done with action.type = "open_page" (or similar)

  These exist in /tmp/codex-proxy-events.ndjson at lines 56894-57842 — we 
  already captured them but our processSseEvent switch ignores these types. So
  they're sitting in the raw log unparsed.

  image_gen is tracked-but-not-invokable from this surface. toolUsage.image_gen
  is on every response envelope (zero counts), but no actual
  image_generation_call SSE item has ever appeared in our logs across 22
  captured turns. Desktop bundle has features.image_generation: !1 (force-off).
  Body shape to invoke = UNKNOWN.

  Neither maps to a known feature flag. They're not gated by
  multi_agent_v2/memories/chronicle/etc. They appear to be unconditionally
  backend-side, with client-side gates only.

  My recommendation for next moves

  1. Cheap, immediate win — instrument web_search. We have the data already.
  Extract the actual SSE shapes from our existing logs, add case 
  "response.web_search_call.*" branches to processSseEvent, capture query,
  sources, results, action.url, build a /debug/web-searches route. ~80 lines, no
   new traffic needed. This turns our existing log corpus into structured intel
  about every search Codex has ever done through the proxy.

  2. Targeted probe — image_gen. The body shape is unknown, but the lever exists
   somewhere. Two angles to try:
  - Fire prompts of the form "Generate an image of X" through Codex CLI and
  watch the wire — the model might naturally invoke image_generation_call if it
  has access
  - Try sending a tools[] entry like {"type": "image_generation"} directly via
  curl with the OAuth token (similar to how the model uses web_search) and see
  if the backend accepts/rejects with a useful error message. The error text
  often reveals what fields are required.

  3. Skip the memory/chronicle probe for now. Codex's section 5 #3 is too vague
  — no clear endpoint, no recovered SSE shapes, just "watch for state
  transitions." Low yield without a concrete target. Park it until we have more
  evidence.

  Suggested order of operations

  a) Extract web_search SSE shapes from the existing logs (5 min, just grep + 
  jq)
  b) Add the proxy hook + /debug/web-searches based on what (a) reveals (~80
  lines + tests)
  c) After that ships, fire one image_gen probe and capture the response — even
  if it errors, the error text + headers will tell us what's needed
  d) Triage from there

  Want me to start with (a)? I can pull the actual SSE event shapes for
  web_search out of /tmp/codex-proxy-events.ndjson right now and you can see
  what's actually flowing before we instrument it.

