  ⎿  Plan saved to: ~/.claude/plans/zazzy-orbiting-matsumoto.md · /plan to edit
     Plan: API-key auth for codex-proxy — server-side OPENAI_API_KEY injection

     Context

     Today the proxy authenticates to upstream by reading ~/.codex/auth.json and
      forwarding the ChatGPT-session OAuth Bearer to
     chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex. This auth path is what your live codex exec
     traffic uses, and it works — but it's locked to your ChatGPT Plus
     subscription's model entitlement, which the backend explicitly gates: "The 
     'gpt-5.1-codex' model is not supported when using Codex with a ChatGPT 
     account." Two of the three models in the gpt-5.1-codex family (-codex,
     -codex-mini) are unreachable from this auth path.

     The proxy already has dual-upstream scaffolding — UpstreamMode = "codex" | 
     "openai", buildOpenAIHeaders, an /openai/* route prefix, and
     OPENAI_UPSTREAM_ORIGIN = "https://api.openai.com". But the existing OpenAI
     path expects the client to supply a Bearer/API-key in the incoming
     authorization or x-api-key header. Codex CLI never sends one — it expects
     the proxy to handle auth — so the OpenAI path is unusable from
     OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3462/v1.

     Goal: make the proxy able to authenticate to api.openai.com with a real API
      key it sources itself, transparently routing Codex CLI traffic to the
     OpenAI direct API so models like gpt-5.1-codex become reachable. Keep OAuth
      as the default; API-key mode is opt-in per-profile and globally
     configurable. All existing profile features (instructions swap, channel
     routing, developer-block control) keep working in API-key mode unchanged.

     Scope

     In:
     - OPENAI_API_KEY env var read at server startup; cached in memory; never
     logged
     - New authMode: "oauth" | "api_key" field on ProfileDef; default still
     "oauth"
     - New global env var CODEX_PROXY_AUTH_MODE setting the default for profiles
      that don't override
     - Server-side API-key injection in the OpenAI upstream path (no client
     Bearer required)
     - Codex-CLI-shape requests (path /v1/responses) automatically route to
     api.openai.com/v1/responses when active profile has authMode: "api_key" —
     no need to add /openai/ prefix or x-codex-proxy-upstream header
     - The 20KB Codex-shaped DEFAULT_INSTRUCTIONS is NOT injected on API-key
     turns (the API doesn't need the ChatGPT-account fiction)
     - The chatgpt-account-id / originator: codex_cli_rs / browser-fingerprint
     headers are NOT sent on API-key turns
     - New GET /debug/auth-mode showing the currently-resolved auth mode + which
      source provided the key (env / profile / file) + whether it loaded
     successfully (NEVER returns the key itself)
     - New example profile openai-direct demonstrating API-key auth with a 5.1
     model
     - Tests for: key resolution priority, header transformation, body
     transformation, upstream URL routing, OAuth fallback when no key is set

     Out:
     - Deleting the OAuth code path. OAuth remains the default; API-key is
     opt-in. You can switch by setting default: "openai-direct" in
     profiles.json, or CODEX_PROXY_AUTH_MODE=api_key globally.
     - Per-key billing / cost tracking. Out of scope for this pass.
     - Streaming-key rotation, keychain integration. Env var + file are enough
     for now.
     - The full developer-tier swap plan from the prior draft — deferred to a
     follow-up after API-key auth lands.

     Architecture

     Auth-mode resolution (priority order)

     1. Active profile's `authMode` field, if set
     2. `CODEX_PROXY_AUTH_MODE` env var (values: "oauth" | "api_key")
     3. Default: "oauth"  (= current behavior)

     This means: if no API key is configured and no profile/env opts in, the
     proxy behaves exactly as today. Existing users see zero change.

     API-key source (priority order)

     1. Active profile's `apiKey` field, if a string is set (raw key in profile
     JSON — only use for ephemeral testing)
     2. `CODEX_PROXY_API_KEY_FILE` env var → read the file's contents, trimmed
     3. `OPENAI_API_KEY` env var
     4. None → fail-fast with a clear log line at request time

     The key is read once at server startup (env var + file are cached).
     Profile-level apiKey is resolved per-request. The cached value is never
     written to any log; only the source identifier is logged ("env" /
     "file:/abs/path" / "profile:openai-direct").

     Upstream routing

     Currently upstreamModeForRequest() returns "openai" only when the path
     starts with /openai/ OR the x-codex-proxy-upstream header is set. Extend
     it: also return "openai" when the active profile's resolved authMode === 
     "api_key". This lets Codex CLI fire to
     OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3462/v1 and the proxy transparently routes
      to api.openai.com.

     The path mapping is straightforward — /v1/responses stays /v1/responses (or
      responses if base path doesn't have /v1). The existing
     buildOpenAIUpstreamUrl() already does this; no change needed.

     Header injection in OpenAI mode

     buildOpenAIHeaders() currently passes through the client's authorization or
      x-api-key if present. Extend it to ALSO accept a resolved API key passed
     in by the caller:

     function buildOpenAIHeaders(source: Headers, injectedApiKey: string | 
     null): Headers

     When injectedApiKey is non-null AND the client did not provide its own
     Bearer/key, set authorization: Bearer ${injectedApiKey}. When the client
     did provide one, prefer the client's (lets curl probes still work via the
     existing /openai/* prefix).

     Headers that don't apply to api.openai.com — chatgpt-account-id,
     originator, oai-product-sku, session_id, browser fingerprint set (origin,
     referer, oai-language, sec-fetch-*) — are already stripped/skipped in
     buildOpenAIHeaders. No change.

     Body transformation in API-key mode

     prepareRequest() currently:
     1. Strips client_metadata, max_output_tokens, max_completion_tokens
     (Codex-backend rejections)
     2. Injects DEFAULT_INSTRUCTIONS if caller didn't supply any
     3. Forces store: false
     4. Normalizes reasoning.summary: "detailed" if FORCE_REASONING_SUMMARY
     5. Scrubs phase and remaps output_text → input_text on echoed input items

     For API-key mode:
     - (1) Keep — these fields are also rejected by api.openai.com. Confirmed
     for max_output_tokens (API requires max_output_tokens to be absent on
     Responses API; not the issue here).
     - (2) Skip the default-instructions injection. The 20KB Codex identity
     prompt only makes sense for the ChatGPT-account upstream. With API-key
     mode, the caller (Codex CLI) supplies its own instructions or none.
     - (3) Keep store: false for symmetry — most callers want non-stored.
     - (4) Keep — reasoning.summary: "detailed" is valid on the OpenAI Responses
      API.
     - (5) Keep — these scrubs are upstream-agnostic.

     prepareRequest() gets a new param: upstreamMode: UpstreamMode. The
     default-instructions branch becomes conditional on upstreamMode === 
     "codex".

     Profile field additions

     // src/profiles.ts
     authMode?: "oauth" | "api_key";

     /**
      * Inline API key. Use only for testing/development — putting `sk-...` in a
      * profile JSON file persists the key to disk. Prefer OPENAI_API_KEY env 
     var
      * or CODEX_PROXY_API_KEY_FILE for anything beyond ephemeral testing.
      */
     apiKey?: string;

     summarizeProfile() reports authMode and hasInlineApiKey: boolean (never the
      key itself).

     New env vars

     CODEX_PROXY_AUTH_MODE       "oauth" | "api_key"   default: "oauth"
     OPENAI_API_KEY              sk-...                cached at startup, never
     logged
     CODEX_PROXY_API_KEY_FILE    /abs/path/to/key      alternative to env var;
     read at startup

     New debug route

     GET /debug/auth-mode returns:

     {
       "defaultAuthMode": "oauth",
       "envAuthMode": "api_key",
       "resolvedDefault": "api_key",
       "apiKey": {
         "configured": true,
         "source": "env",
         "preview": "sk-…ABC",
         "length": 51,
         "loadedAt": "2026-05-11T01:00:00Z"
       },
       "oauth": {
         "available": true,
         "accountId": "80aa022c-…",
         "expiresAt": "2026-05-11T05:00:00Z",
         "scopes": ["openai.codex"]
       },
       "profilesWithApiKeyOverride": ["openai-direct"]
     }

     preview is sk- + first 3 chars after the hyphen + ellipsis + last 3 chars.
     The full key NEVER appears in any response. The route is purely diagnostic
     — confirms the key loaded, where it came from, and what its provenance is.

     Example profile

     "openai-direct": {
       "authMode": "api_key",
       "writeRoles": ["system", "developer", "user"],
       "tools": "*",
       "readChannels": ["final", "analysis", "commentary"],
       "bodyOverrides": {
         "reasoning.effort": "high",
         "reasoning.summary": "detailed"
       }
     }

     Selecting this profile (via the existing x-codex-proxy-profile header, or
     by setting it as default in profiles.json) routes the turn to
     api.openai.com with the env-supplied API key. Pair with -m gpt-5.1-codex on
      Codex CLI to verify 5.1 unlocks.

     Forwarding flow (final shape)

     For each /v1/responses or /responses POST:

     1. identifyProfile()       → profile
     2. resolveAuthMode(profile)
     3. if authMode === "oauth":
          same path as today (buildHeaders + getAuthState + chatgpt-backend
     upstream)
     4. if authMode === "api_key":
          a. resolveApiKey(profile)         — env / file / profile
          b. if no key → 503 with helpful error
          c. prepareRequest(body, "api_key") — skip default-instructions
     injection
          d. applyProfileGates(body, profile) — unchanged
          e. buildOpenAIHeaders(headers, apiKey)
          f. fetch(buildOpenAIUpstreamUrl(req.url), { headers, body })
          g. stream the response back through interceptStream()
                                               — channel routing, envelope
     capture,
                                                 refusal detection, etc. all
     unchanged

     Critical observation: the proxy's analysis layer (channels, envelope,
     harmony log, debug routes) is upstream-agnostic. It operates on the SSE
     event stream, which has the same shape from both upstreams. Everything
     we've built for observability keeps working in API-key mode without
     changes.

