  What works:

  Layer: resolveApiKey() priority chain — profile > file > ~/.codex/auth.json >
    env
  Status: ✅ Live, masked-preview verified
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: auth.json API-key reader (handles auth_mode: "apikey" writes from codex
   
    login)
  Status: ✅ Picked up real sk-or-... key
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: authMode: "api_key" profile field forces upstream routing
  Status: ✅ openai-direct profile triggers api.openai.com
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: Path rewrite /backend-api/codex/responses → /v1/responses
  Status: ✅ Codex-shape input → OpenAI-shape output
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: Server-side API-key injection in buildOpenAIHeaders
  Status: ✅ Bearer auto-attached; client header still wins if present
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: DEFAULT_INSTRUCTIONS injection guarded on upstreamMode === "codex"
  Status: ✅ Not injected on api.openai.com calls
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: chatgpt-account-id / originator / oai-product-sku stripped in OpenAI
    mode
  Status: ✅ Wire-verified clean
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: Fail-fast 503 when api_key mode + no key
  Status: ✅
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: /debug/auth-mode route (no key leak; preview only)
  Status: ✅ Returns mode + masked preview + source
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Layer: Regression: existing OAuth path unchanged
  Status: ✅ Test suite green; no production code change to the OAuth branch

  Live evidence the path-rewrite + auth-injection worked: the 401 came from
  OpenAI itself (request id req_dc0fbf6bd4334f44..., cf-ray 9f9f8b05...-SOF),
  saying "Incorrect API key provided: sk-or-v1..."* — that's an OpenRouter key
  sitting in your ~/.codex/auth.json (which is why OpenAI rejected it). The
  proxy did its job; the upstream rejected the credential.

  To unlock gpt-5.1-codex you need to swap the key in ~/.codex/auth.json to a 
  real OpenAI API key (or set OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-... directly with a real key —
  the proxy will pick whichever has higher priority). Three concrete paths:

  1. codex login --api-key sk-... → rewrites ~/.codex/auth.json cleanly
  (auth_mode=apikey)
  2. Edit ~/.codex/auth.json manually, replace the OPENAI_API_KEY field
  3. export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-<real> + clear the auth.json key field (env is
  lower priority than auth.json, so the file wins)

  Files changed

  - src/auth.ts — added resolveApiKey, loadAuthFileKey, key caching, preview
  masking
  - src/server.ts — prepareRequest(upstreamMode), resolveAuthMode, profile-aware
   upstreamModeForRequest, buildOpenAIHeaders(key), path rewrite in
  normalizeOpenAIPath, /debug/auth-mode route, api_key-mode wiring in main
  handler with fail-fast 503
  - src/profiles.ts — authMode + apiKey fields on ProfileDef, summarizer update
  - src/server.test.ts — 16 new tests covering resolution priority, header
  injection, path rewrite, upstream-mode logic, instruction-injection guard
  - examples/profiles.json + ~/.codex-proxy/profiles.json — openai-direct
  profile

  Net change: ~290 lines added across 5 files. No deletions to existing OAuth
  code path.

  The deferred work (full developer-tier swap with replaceDeveloperBlocks/inject
  DeveloperBlocks/nukeDeveloperItems/stuffAssistantHistory + /debug/final-body)
  is still queued and now upstream-agnostic — will work in both auth modes
  whenever you want to pick it up.

  Want me to verify with a real key once you swap it in, or move on to the
  deferred swap plan?

