⏺ OpenRouter changes things — different origin, slightly different conventions.
  Two paths, ordered by friction:

  Quick test (env var, no code): OpenRouter exposes the OpenAI-compatible
  /api/v1/responses endpoint and accepts Authorization: Bearer sk-or-v1-.... The
   proxy already has an env override:

  export CODEX_PROXY_OPENAI_UPSTREAM_ORIGIN=https://openrouter.ai/api
  export CODEX_PROXY_OPENAI_UPSTREAM_BASE_PATH=/v1
  # (your sk-or-v1-... is already in ~/.codex/auth.json)
  # restart proxy + set default profile to openai-direct

  That'd flip the entire OpenAI upstream to OpenRouter globally. Works for
  one-shot testing. Downside: it's global — every api_key-mode request goes to
  OpenRouter, not just one profile.

  Per-profile (cleaner, ~30 lines): add upstreamOrigin / upstreamBasePath /
  extraHeaders to ProfileDef so you can have both openai-direct (api.openai.com)
   AND openrouter (openrouter.ai) profiles side by side. OpenRouter also wants
  HTTP-Referer + X-Title headers (optional but they rate-limit harder without
  them), which the extraHeaders mechanism would handle.

  Two specific things to verify before we wire it:
  1. Does OpenRouter pass gpt-5.1-codex through to OpenAI? Their convention is
  openai/gpt-5.1-codex (prefixed). If unprefixed routes ambiguously, we may need
   a tiny model-rewrite knob too.
  2. Does OpenRouter expose /v1/responses? They added Responses API support
  recently but their docs lead with /v1/chat/completions. If /responses 404s,
