⏺ Done. The full path is live.

  Result

  gpt-5.1-codex is now reachable through your OpenRouter key via the proxy. Both
   model name forms work:

  Model name sent: gpt-5.1-codex (unprefixed)
  OpenRouter routed to: openai/gpt-5.1-codex-20251113
  Result: ✅ "router unprefixed ok"
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Model name sent: openai/gpt-5.1-codex (prefixed)
  OpenRouter routed to: openai/gpt-5.1-codex-20251113
  Result: ✅ "router prefixed ok"

  Bonus intel: OpenRouter resolved your request to the 20251113 version pin of
  gpt-5.1-codex (date-stamped snapshot, mid-Nov 2025).

  What needed changing for OpenRouter (beyond yesterday's plan)

  One bug — buildOpenAIUpstreamUrl was clobbering origin path prefixes. When you
   set OPENAI_UPSTREAM_ORIGIN=https://openrouter.ai/api, the proxy was building
  https://openrouter.ai/v1/responses (dropping /api) instead of
  https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/responses. Fixed in src/server.ts:549-571 — the
  function now preserves the origin's path prefix and appends the mapped request
   path.

  Tests stay 176/176 green.

  To make OpenRouter the persistent default

  Add to ~/.zshrc (so the proxy picks them up on next launchd restart):

  export CODEX_PROXY_OPENAI_UPSTREAM_ORIGIN=https://openrouter.ai/api
  export CODEX_PROXY_OPENAI_UPSTREAM_BASE_PATH=/v1

  And ensure the openai-direct profile gets selected. Either:
  - Set default: "openai-direct" in ~/.codex-proxy/profiles.json (all traffic
  uses OpenRouter)
  - Or keep director as default and use x-codex-proxy-profile: openai-direct
  header for selective opt-in (Codex CLI doesn't support custom headers cleanly
  though)
  - Or split-the-decision: keep your OAuth ChatGPT-Plus path for normal work,
  fire the OpenRouter path only when you explicitly want 5.1

  Things to be aware of

  - safetyIdentifier, toolUsage, moderation fields are absent from OpenRouter
  responses (envelope shows them as null). OpenRouter strips/doesn't pass these
  from OpenAI. So debug routes that rely on those signals are quieter in
  OpenRouter mode.
  - Rate-limit headers (x-codex-*) won't fire because OpenRouter doesn't emit
  them — they're ChatGPT-account-specific. /debug/ratelimits will be empty for
  OpenRouter sessions.
  - Cost tracking: every successful OpenRouter call hits your OpenRouter
  balance. The proxy doesn't track per-request cost; you'd watch openrouter.ai's
   dashboard.
  - HTTP-Referer + X-Title headers aren't being sent. OpenRouter rate-limits
  stricter without them. If you start hitting limits, we can add an extraHeaders
   field to ProfileDef (~10 lines) and ship those.

  What's still queued

  The deferred developer-tier swap plan (replaceDeveloperBlocks /
  injectDeveloperBlocks / nukeDeveloperItems / stuffAssistantHistory /
  /debug/final-body) — now it'd work in both OAuth and OpenRouter modes since
  the swap primitives are upstream-agnostic.

  Want me to add extraHeaders for HTTP-Referer/X-Title (small now, before the
  swap plan), or jump straight to the swap work?

