  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Field: moderation
  chatgpt.com (OAuth, gpt-5.5): absent on both
  OpenRouter → OpenAI (gpt-5.1-codex): absent on both
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Field: Response shape (SSE event stream)
  chatgpt.com (OAuth, gpt-5.5): Identical — same Harmony channels, same
    response.completed envelope, same encrypted_content reasoning blobs
  OpenRouter → OpenAI (gpt-5.1-codex):

  The deltas worth knowing:

  1. Your safetyIdentifier disappears. ChatGPT-backend stamps every request with
   user-<base64> — a per-user safety-classifier handle the backend uses to
  remember your account-level history. OpenAI's direct API (which OpenRouter
  forwards to) doesn't return this for API-key auth. That's a lost observability
   signal — when you're on the api_key path, you can't tell whether the
  moderation classifier is being lenient or strict toward you specifically.
  2. toolUsage block goes silent. ChatGPT-backend reports zero counters for
  image_gen and web_search on every turn — those are server-side capabilities a
  ChatGPT-account user CAN invoke (we found those as "shadow tools" earlier).
  OpenAI-API-direct doesn't emit this block. So the shadow-tool intel only flows
   through the OAuth path.
  3. promptCacheRetention disappears. ChatGPT-backend tells you it caches your
  prompt for 24h. OpenAI direct doesn't expose this. You're likely still being
  prompt-cached on OpenAI's side (their docs say so) but the wire doesn't tell
  you the TTL.
  4. OpenRouter rewrites the model name to its canonical openai/<model>-<date>
  form. Useful — you see what version got served.
  5. Codex's identity stamping is gone. No more chatgpt-account-id header on
  outbound, no originator: codex_cli_rs (the proxy strips both for
  api.openai.com). Your account fingerprint shrinks.
  6. Default parallelToolCalls differs. chatgpt-backend defaults to true, OpenAI
   direct returns false. If you rely on parallel tool calls and your client
  doesn't explicitly set it, behavior may differ.
  7. Rate-limit headers disappear. x-codex-active-limit,
  x-codex-primary-used-percent, etc. — the entire x-codex-* family is
  ChatGPT-account-specific. /debug/ratelimits shows nothing for OpenRouter
  sessions. You don't see usage % until you hit a wall.
  8. Cyber-policy gate replaced with OpenAI's classifier. Our cyber_policy
  detection (Trusted Access for Cyber) won't fire — that gate is
  ChatGPT-account-only. OpenAI direct uses different moderation codes
  (policy_violation, generic invalid_request, etc.) which isCyberPolicyCode does
   cover, so blocks still flag — but the metadata payload
  openai_verification_recommendation: ["trusted_access_for_cyber"] we caught
  yesterday won't appear.
  9. OpenRouter adds its own headers (worth instrumenting eventually): x-or-*,
  possibly cost / credit tracking. They're flowing into /debug/headers right now
   if you query during an OpenRouter session.

  Net trade:
  - You gain: model entitlement (5.1 family), a less account-fingerprinted
  upstream, billing by token usage
  - You lose: safety-identifier observability, shadow-tool counters,
  prompt-cache TTL surfacing, rate-limit telemetry, cyber-policy metadata
  - Identical on both: the actual SSE event flow (channels, reasoning
  encrypted_content blobs, refusal patterns), tool calling, body shape

  Want me to add an OpenRouter-specific header capture so x-or-* flows into
  /debug/headers cleanly and we can see what new observability OpenRouter offers
   in return for what we lost?

