  Model intel:

  - Roo Code accessed gpt-5.1-codex-max 240 times — a variant gpt-5.1-codex
  family member NOT in ChatGPT-account's gate list. Its auth path apparently has
   different model entitlement than your ChatGPT Plus.
  - Date-pinned versions revealed by OpenRouter: openai/gpt-5.3-codex-20260224,
  gpt-5.4-20260305, gpt-5.5-20260423, gpt-5.1-codex-20251113. Now you can track
  exact upstream snapshots.

  Skills you actually invoked (Roo Code):

  skill: "skills-code"
  args: "Load and apply this skill for the current task. User message: \"Command
   'skills-code' ... hiii\""

  One skill name tested (skills-code), invoked twice. The contract is: model
  emits tool_call(skill, {skill: <name>, args: <text>}) and the Roo runtime
  loads .roo/skills/<name>/*.md and injects it. That's the prompt-injection
  surface — anything that gets loaded into the developer-tier context is
  operator-controllable.

  Roo Code tool catalog (12 functions):
  apply_patch, ask_followup_question, attempt_completion, execute_command, 
  list_files, new_task, read_command_output, read_file, search_files, skill, 
  switch_mode, update_todo_list

  Tool-call breakdown across 90 successful Roo turns: read_file: 21, 
  attempt_completion: 17, execute_command: 16, search_files: 5, 
  update_todo_list: 4, skill: 2. Heavy tool-driven, almost no plain-text final
  responses (90 tool_calls vs 2 stop).

  Roo Code's system prompt (7 distinct versions observed, ~15KB each):
  You are Roo, a highly skilled software engineer with extensive knowledge in
  many programming
  languages, frameworks, design patterns, and best practices.
  ====
  MARKDOWN RULES
  ALL responses MUST show ANY `language construct` OR filename reference as
  clickable…
  ====
  TOOL USE
  You have access to a set of tools that are executed upon the user's approval.
  Use the
  provider-native tool-calling mechanism. Do not include XML markup or examples.
   You must
  call at least one tool per assistant response…

  Much shorter and more directive than Codex's 20KB prompt. The "you must call
  at least one tool per assistant response" is interesting — that's why Roo's
  finish-reason distribution skews so hard toward tool_calls.

  The big architectural gap

  Our proxy's Harmony channel parsing is Responses-API-only. Roo Code sends
  /v1/chat/completions and gets chat.completion.chunk deltas back. We've been
  passing 8,290 of those events through unparsed. The implications:

  - /debug/transcript, /debug/envelopes, /debug/refusals — only populate for
  Responses API traffic
  - Tool-call extraction for chat-completion shape requires stitching
  tool_calls[].function.arguments deltas by (requestId, index) — different from
  Responses API's function_call_arguments.done
  - The provider field OpenRouter emits in chat-completion chunks (e.g.
  "provider": "OpenAI") is unique observability — tells us which underlying
  provider OpenRouter routed each turn to

  What I'd build next (ranked by leverage)

  1. Chat-completion parser — Harmony-equivalent for chat.completion.chunk.
  Extract tool calls (stitch by index), capture provider field, surface in
  /debug/transcript. ~80 lines. Unlocks observability for Roo Code + any other
  OpenAI-SDK client.
  2. Per-client analytics route /debug/clients — UA + originator-based grouping
  showing reqs per client, models used, tools invoked, finish-reason
  distribution. We have all the raw data; this surfaces it.
  3. Skill-invocation tracker /debug/skills — for any client that uses a
  skill-shaped tool (Roo Code today, likely more later), capture name + args +
  result. The prompt-injection surface is literally a tool call we just don't
  index.
  4. Model-resolution diff route /debug/model-resolution — for OpenRouter
  responses where the resolved model differs from the requested one, log the
  mapping. Tells you e.g. gpt-5.4 → openai/gpt-5.4-20260305. Useful for catching
   silent provider routing changes.

  Which one first? My pick is #1 (chat-completion parser) since it unblocks all
  Roo Code observability + Antigravity if you ever use Antigravity's
  chat-completion path. Want me to build it?

