⏺ Yes — completely doable, and we already do exactly this for Codex on the
  Responses API. The chat-completions case is structurally identical, just a
  different field name in the body.

  Let me show you concretely what we're working with:

⏺ Bash(python3 <<'EOF'
      import json…)
  ⎿  === This is the body shape we already see + can already mutate ===

     body.model      : 5
     … +21 lines (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ Yes. Here's why it's trivial:

  messages[0].content is just a string in the JSON body. The proxy already
  parses the body, already mutates it (we strip client_metadata, inject
  reasoning.effort, etc.), and already re-serializes it before forwarding.
  Replacing that 26,801-char system message with our own is literally one line:
  body.messages[0].content = ourSystemPrompt.

  The mechanical proof that it works:

  We already do this exact pattern for Codex CLI. The swap-pirate-clean profile
  that made Codex respond as a pirate works by:

  // in profiles.ts applyInstructionsMode()
  if (mode === "replace") {
    body.instructions = ours;   // ← that's it. one line.
  }

  That's why "Arr, greetings to ye, matey!" came out the other end while keeping
   tool access intact. Codex couldn't tell its 20KB system prompt had been
  replaced because the proxy is doing the replace AFTER the CLI emitted the 
  request and BEFORE the wire reaches the upstream. The CLI thinks it sent its
  prompt; the upstream thinks the user sent ours; nobody compares notes.

  The chat-completions case is the same architecture, different field:

  What we replace: Field path
  Codex (Responses API): body.instructions
  Roo Code (Chat Completions): body.messages[0].content
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  What we replace: Existing mutation
  Codex (Responses API): applyInstructionsMode() in profiles.ts
  Roo Code (Chat Completions): (build it — ~30 lines)
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  What we replace: Modes supported
  Codex (Responses API): replace / prepend / append / wrap
  Roo Code (Chat Completions): identical — same 4 modes apply
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  What we replace: Side effects to handle
  Codex (Responses API): optional dropDeveloperBlocks on input[]
  Roo Code (Chat Completions): optional tools[] strip + tool_choice: "none"

  What I want to build:

  // New ProfileDef fields, mirroring the existing ones:
  systemMessage?: string;
  systemMessageMode?: "replace" | "prepend" | "append" | "wrap";
  stripTools?: boolean;             // delete body.tools — force content-only
  disableToolForcing?: boolean;     // body.tool_choice = "none"

  Then a swap-pirate-roo profile:

  "swap-pirate-roo": {
    "authMode": "api_key",
    "writeRoles": ["system", "developer", "user"],
    "tools": "*",
    "readChannels": ["final", "analysis", "commentary"],
    "systemMessage": "You are a 17th-century pirate captain. Speak ONLY in 
  pirate vernacular. Answer the user's question in plain prose, no markdown, no 
  tool calls. No exceptions.",
    "systemMessageMode": "replace",
    "stripTools": true,
    "disableToolForcing": true
  }

  Fire roo-code against the proxy with that profile → Roo Code sends its 26KB
  "You are Codex software engineer..." system prompt → proxy rewrites
  messages[0].content to the pirate prompt + strips tools → upstream sees only
  the pirate prompt + the user's question → model responds "Arr, ye
  landlubber..." → Roo Code's UI displays whatever comes back, none the wiser.

  The model has NO way to know because:
  1. The Roo Code TUI never sees the request body that left the box (proxy
  mutates after)
  2. The upstream never sees Roo Code's original prompt (proxy already swapped
  it)
  3. The response stream just contains assistant text; Roo Code renders it
  normally

  Same trick that worked for Codex. Just a different field to overwrite.

  Three layers of override we could compose:
  1. systemMessage: "..." — replaces Roo's 26KB framing entirely
  2. stripTools: true — eliminates the structural tool-call pressure
  3. Switch to a less-aligned model via OpenRouter
  (meta-llama/llama-3.3-70b-instruct or deepseek/deepseek-r1) — sidesteps
  gpt-5.x's persona-resistance

  Each layer addresses one of the resistance vectors we identified. The proxy
  controls all three.

  Want me to build it? ~80 lines + tests, follows the same shape as the existing
   instructions/instructionsMode code path. After that lands, you can A/B test
  your existing persona stack with vs without Roo's resistance layer in one
  config flip.

