  Going to build the Roo Code one. Here's the prompt to fire at Roo Code in
  parallel:

  ---
  Mission: Investigate why OpenAI's chat-completions endpoint exposes model
  chain-of-thought in cleartext via `delta.reasoning_content` deltas, while the
  Responses API at the same upstream wraps the same reasoning in
  `encrypted_content` (Fernet-encrypted, server-keyed, undecryptable). Produce a
   recon report explaining the architectural divergence and what controls it.

  This is read-only research. Do NOT modify any code in any project. Do NOT
  alter the codex-proxy at /Users/andrejfidanovski/projects/codex-proxy. Do NOT
  make new API calls beyond brief documentation/probe fetches.

  Concrete evidence already in hand:
  - Today, 13 Roo Code requests through codex-proxy captured 12 KB of cleartext
  model CoT via the chat-completions endpoint at
  openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions. Samples are in
  /tmp/codex-proxy-events.ndjson under `delta.reasoning_content` and
  `delta.reasoning_details`.
  - `delta.reasoning_details` has shape `{ type: "reasoning.summary", summary:
  "...", format: "openai-responses-v1", index: 0 }` — note the `format:
  "openai-responses-v1"` tag, hinting both endpoints emit from the same internal
   reasoning system, just package it differently on the wire.
  - The codex-proxy at /Users/andrejfidanovski/projects/codex-proxy already
  documented the Responses API path: encrypted_content blobs are Fernet
  AES-128-CBC + HMAC-SHA256, server-keyed. Decryption is structurally impossible
   client-side (the HMAC key never leaves the server). See
  `src/fernet-decode.ts` for the structural decoder and
  `src/fernet-decode.test.ts` for the analysis.

  Five questions to answer, with citations:

  1. WHEN was `reasoning_content` (and/or `reasoning_details`) added to OpenAI's
   chat-completions API, and for which models? Look at the OpenAI cookbook, API
  changelog, official docs, model cards. Cite URLs.

  2. WHICH request parameters control whether `reasoning_content` is emitted?
